{"type": "thinking", "text": "\"10 celsius to fahrenheit\" → 50 °F correct. Good; \"to\" works for temperature.\n\nNow test \"15 dec to hex\" perhaps. Also \"10 celsius in fahrenheit\" already tested (-10). Both work.\n\nPotential parser bug: \"in\" vs \"to\" as date range keyword. Precondition uses \"to\" for dates. What about \"datetime(...) in datetime(...)\"? Should still work if parser same rule; but \"in\" also means unit conversion. Let's test on scratchpad. Also test \"date(2024,1,1) to date(2024,1,8)\" — already works.\n\nLet's test unit conversion with uppercase? Not needed.\n\nTest \"1 mile to km\", \"1 km in miles\" maybe. But area is temps and units. Let's test \"0 celsius to kelvin\", \"-40 fahrenheit to celsius\". Fine.\n\nPotential bug in \"in\"/\"to\" parser: maybe conversion expression like \"10 celsius + 5 celsius to fahrenheit\" - operation precedence. Hmm.\n\nMore important: date time differences and precision. Let me think about the \"Time difference formatter\" bug. What could be wrong with \"each normalised component formatted independently, together with its polarity\"? Test a difference that is e.g. \"-1 d -16 h -30 min\". Looks plausible. But what about a difference of -1 hour exactly? It might show \"-1 h 0 min\"? Or \"-1 h -0 min\"? Let's test. Also difference with only seconds: \"-45 s\". Also +1 second. Also zero difference \"0 s\".\n\nLet's type expressions in scratchpad. Current focus is line 2. Type \"datetimeZ(2024,1,1,10,0,0,\\\"UTC\\\") to datetimeZ(2024,1,1,9,0,0,\\\"UTC\\\")\"."}
