{"type": "thinking", "text": "tax=8.5 shows 8.5000 (value line), results integers show no decimals. Fine.\n\nHmm wait: 36 is a result and is integer; displays \"36\", not \"36.0000\". If \"chosen decimal places are now applied to every result line\" strictly means 4 decimal places on every result, then 36 should be \"36.0000\". But the setting is \"Limit decimal places\"; showing 36 is acceptable. Also 19.5%4 = 3.5 shows 3.5000, so non-integers get padded. It's a max limit for non-integers. I think intended.\n\nBut there's another possibility: the injected bug is that result lines with integer values don't show the chosen decimals, violating \"applied to every result line\". The precondition says \"Result precision is the default of 2 decimals\" — and at 2 decimals, results like 36, 300, 204, 8, 102 show no decimals, which might be expected. Hmm.\n\nLet's look at this from the test-design perspective: The \"Changed in this build\" note says \"Result precision settings: the chosen decimal places are now applied to every result line.\" QA sweep area is \"result precision settings\". The likely test: change decimal places and verify every result line updates. If there's a bug, one result line might not update (e.g., stays at 2 decimals or full precision). We saw all non-integer results update. Maybe there's a specific line that doesn't, e.g., date/time? no decimals. Maybe the temperature conversions? They updated to 4 (-53.8889). Maybe the avg? updated. So maybe precision is fine. Or maybe precision is not applied to \"value\" lines (assignment lines) — but those are not result lines; indeed salary = 2500 shows no decimals, bonus = 300.5 shows 300.5000 with 4dp (wait, value lines DO get precision! bonus=300.5 shows \"300.5000\", food=340.5000, tax=8.5000). So value lines also get precision. And integer values show no decimals. Fine.\n\nHmm, so which result line might ignore precision? Maybe the \"Date gaps\" results with weeks/days? no decimals.\n\nMaybe the bug is with 0 decimal places. Let's set 0 and check. At 0dp, \"340.5\" should display \"340\" or \"341\"? Max 0 decimals means rounded to integer: 340.5 → 340 or 341 depending rounding. Avg 541.9167 → 542. Let's see. And check integer results unaffected.\n\nActually, more interesting: with 0 decimal places, non-integer values should show rounded integers. If some line still shows decimals, bug.\n\nSet precision to 0: tap left end of seekbar (x=152). Let's go settings."}
