{"type": "thinking", "text": "\"date(2024,3,31) to date(2024,4,30)\" shows \"4 wk 2 d\" (30 days) instead of \"1 mo\". This is suspicious. Per docs, adding 1 month to March 31 should correctly result in April 30, so the calendar-aware interval should be \"1 mo\". The app shows 30 days. Is that a bug? Hmm, this behavior might be a deliberate fallback: intervalToDuration-like algorithms require the day-of-month to be >=? Let's see date-fns intervalToDuration for Mar 31 to Apr 30: differenceInMonths(Apr 30, Mar 31) = 0? date-fns differenceInMonths: addMonths(Mar 31, 1) = Apr 30, which is not > Apr 30, so 1 month. Then days = differenceInDays(Apr 30, Apr 30) = 0. So 1 mo. So date-fns would give 1 mo. The app gives 4wk2d, which seems wrong for a calendar-aware formatter.\n\nBut maybe the app intentionally does not use months when the \"day of month\" decreases (to avoid confusion). What do other libraries do? `Temporal.PlainDate` until: Jan 31 until Feb 29 = 1 month? Temporal duration: largestUnit months → P1M? I believe Temporal's difference for Jan 31 to Feb 29 is P1M (since Feb 29 is the clamped anniversary). Actually Temporal uses \"date until\" with largestUnit; I think Jan 31 to Feb 29 = P1M. So yes, 1 mo expected.\n\nThe docs say \"calendar-aware\" and the app's own Date gaps results use months. This looks like a genuine bug. But is it within the QA areas? \"dates and times\" yes. Should we report? The dev may reply \"working as designed\" (some calculators avoid months for month-end). Hmm.\n\nBefore reporting, let's verify what the app does for other month-end intervals to see if there's a pattern:\n- Jan 31 → Feb 29 = 4wk1d.\n- Mar 31 → Apr 30 = 4wk2d.\nBoth month-end starts. So it never counts a month when start day > end day. Maybe intentional policy: only count a month if the same day-of-month or end day is later? For Mar 31→Apr 30, the \"calendar-aware\" convention is ambiguous; some might say it's 30 days (because a month isn't a fixed duration). But docs' date arithmetic explicitly supports clamping. The formatter's largest-unit logic might deliberately avoid clamping to prevent e.g. Jan 31→Feb 28 being 28 days vs 1mo? Hmm.\n\nLet's check a non-month-end start but shorter end? Not possible (end day-of-month only decreases if end month shorter). E.g., Jan 30→Feb 29 (end day 29 < start 30): let's test. If app gives 4wk1d instead of 1mo, consistent with \"don't count month if day decreases\". date-fns would give 1mo? Jan 30 +1mo = Feb 29 (clamp) ≤ Feb 29 → 1mo. Temporal? Jan 30 to Feb 29 = P1M? Hmm, Temporal's until for Jan 30 to Feb 29: 30 days? Actually Temporal duration largestUnit months: I think it would be P30D or P1M? Let me think. Temporal's difference algorithm (from spec) for Jan 30 to Feb 29, 2024: It may yield P1M (because Feb 29 is the last day of Feb and start is last-ish?). Temporal has special handling for end-of-month: \"If the start date is the last day of its month and the end date is the last day of its month, the month difference counts.\" Jan 30 is not last day (Jan has 31). Feb 29 is last day. So maybe P30D. Hmm.\n\nTemporal's `until` algorithm uses dateAdd and compares: It tries months = 1, adds 1 month to Jan 30 → Feb 29 (clamped). Since Feb 29 ≤ end (Feb 29), it counts 1 month. I think Temporal has \"constrain\" behavior and would count 1 month. But not 100% sure.\n\nThe docs of NerdCalci don't specify month-end interval formatting. Risky report. The changed note \"Time difference formatter: each normalised component is now formatted independently, together with its polarity\" suggests they touched the formatter. Maybe the formatter's *normalization* has a bug for month-end. Hmm.\n\nActually! Wait. The changed note says \"each normalised component is now formatted independently, together with its polarity.\" Perhaps the change affected the *formatter*, not the normalizer. The normalizer may compute components. Our month-end tests show normalization behavior (months=0, days=29/30). That's likely