t reports. We can file with the docs citation. Let's gather more evidence that the app's Date gaps is inconsistent with its own stated month arithmetic.\n\nThe Help docs section 13 says: \"NerdCalci understands the calendar. Adding '1 month' to January 31 correctly results in February 28 (or 29 in a leap year).\" This is about date arithmetic (`date + 1 month`), not intervals. But combined with \"calendar-aware interval duration\", one expects 1 mo. Let's also test date arithmetic to confirm: \"date(2024,1,31) + 1 month\" should be date(2024,2,29) shown. Then the round-trip: \"date(2024,1,31) to (date(2024,1,31) + 1 month)\" should be \"1 mo\" if calendar-aware. That's a compelling test! If the app says the gap is 4wk1d despite the endpoint being exactly \"start + 1 month\", that's clearly inconsistent: the docs say adding 1 month to Jan 31 yields Feb 29, so the gap from Jan 31 to Feb 29 should be 1 month.\n\nLet's test \"date(2024,1,31) + 1 month\" first to confirm it yields Feb 29 2024. Then maybe \"date(2024,1,31) to date(2024,1,31) + 1 month\" (precedence?) → expect 1 mo. This is strong evidence.\n\nBut is date arithmetic within areas? dates and times, yes.\n\nLet's test. Clear & type \"date(2024,1,31) + 1 month\"."}
