{"type": "thinking", "text": "Whoa! \"date(2020,2,29) to date(2021,2,28)\" gives \"11 mo 30 d\". That's not 1 year, but is it wrong? Feb 29 2020 to Feb 28 2021 = 365 days. The calendar-aware decomposition could be \"11 mo 30 d\" (Feb 29 2020 + 11 months = Jan 29 2021, +30 days = Feb 28 2021). That's internally consistent! Many libraries (date-fns) would give 11 months 30 days or 1 year 0 days? Let's check date-fns intervalToDuration: start Feb 29 2020, end Feb 28 2021. differenceInYears: addYears(Feb 29 2020, 1) = Feb 28 2021 (clamps) → equals end, so years=1, months=0, days=0. So date-fns gives 1 year. Temporal might give 11 months 30 days? Hmm.\n\nThis shows the app uses a decomposition based on days/months where the day-of-month rule prevents counting the final month when the end day is 28 < start day 29. But it gave \"11 mo 30 d\": from Feb 29 2020 + 11 months = Jan 29 2021, +30 days = Feb 28 2021. That works; so it counts months while end day >= start day? Let's see: 11 months lands Jan 29 (day 29 = start day 29), then remaining 30 days. If it counted 12 months, that would clamp to Feb 28 2021 (day 28 < 29), which it refuses. So the algorithm requires the day-of-month match (with clamping not allowed). This is a consistent policy: only count full months when the day-of-month is equal. That's actually how many simple \"difference in months\" functions work (e.g., moment.diff(..., 'months') returns 11 for Feb 29→Feb 28? Moment's diff months gives 12? Not sure). \n\nAnyway, the app's \"calendar-aware\" behavior is a design choice. Reporting it as a bug is uncertain. The docs say \"Adding '1 month' to January 31 correctly results in February 28 (or 29 in a leap year)\" which contradicts the interval's non-clamping policy. But maybe the docs only describe arithmetic, not interval decomposition.\n\nHmm, I worry about false positives. Let's instead test the *changed* areas more precisely. The sweep areas are three: dates/times, temperatures/units, result precision. We found one confirmed temp bug. Maybe there's also a distinct bug in the \"Temperature\" conversions file (not sums) related to unit keyword \"in\"/\"to\"? All four conversions correct.\n\nWait, let's check -65 °F in °C at 2dp: -53.89 (correct rounding of -53.888... to 2 decimals is -53.89). At 4dp -53.8889. Good.\n\nCheck \"Temperature sums\" third row expected per docs 68 °F, observed 527.67 °F. Same bug.\n\nMaybe test \"35 celsius + 10 fahrenheit in celsius\" per docs → 40.56 °C. Current build will give 564.7? Actually absolute: 35C=308.15K, 10F=260.928K, sum 569.078K; in C = 295.928°C. Let's compute display: \"35 celsius + 10 fahrenheit in celsius\" might parse as arithmetic then convert? Result unit from RHS? The parser precedence maybe (35C + 10F) in C = 295.93°C. That's part of same bug. No new report.\n\nNow, let's test result precision with more edge cases that could be the injected bug:\n- The chosen decimal places applied to every result line: maybe *variables/assignment results* should reflect precision, and they do. \n- Maybe the bug is that precision is applied to *temperature* results? It is.\n- Maybe the bug is that precision isn't applied to *date/time query decimals* (e.g., \"hours since ...\" returns many decimals). The changelog says \"decimal precision for date/time queries\". With precision 2, \"hours between datetime(...)\" showed 30.50 h (okay). What about \"days since datetime(2024,1,1,10,0,0)\" with current date 2026-11-14? Should be a decimal number of days. Let's test! If precision setting not applied, it might show full precision. Area: dates/times + precision. Could be the bug.\n\nTest \"days since datetimeZ(2024,1,1,10,0,0,\\\"UTC\\\")\" → should be ~1048.xx d with 2 decimals. Let's see. Clear & type."}
